# The slide

Topic 13

After this talk you can:

- measure a tick without letting the core read a clock
- shed work in levels when the budget is short
- explain why a long single-process session finds what short runs miss

---

## The symptom

3.0 and 3.1 started near 85.7 APS and lost 5 to 8 points within hours on live clients. The bench could not reproduce it.

3.1's first full pass, hour by hour (L-22):

| Hour | APS | Survival |
|---|---|---|
| first | 85.7 | 91.8% |
| second | 82.1 | 85.2% |
| third | 79.7 | 79.1% |
| last 40 min | 78.6 | 76.1% |

The opponent strength was constant. It finished 65th after being 16th in its first hour.

Source: `data/learnings.md` L-22, README.

---

## First defence: a tick budget

Robocode skips a robot's turn if it takes too long. A skipped turn can mean a missed shot, a missed scan, or a lost round.

Hadur times its own core call in the adapter, and hands the measurement in as an event on the next tick.

```java
if (lastTickNanos >= 0)
    pending.add(0, new BotEvent.TickTime(lastTickNanos, TICK_ALLOWANCE_NANOS));
BotInput in = input();
long start = System.nanoTime();
BotOrders orders = guard.tick(in);
lastTickNanos = System.nanoTime() - start;
```

(`hadur2/Hadur.java`.) The adapter may read the clock. The core may not (RES-6), so a replay sees the same times (CORE-2).

---

## Shed work in levels (TIME-1, TIME-2)

```java
public static int wavesToSurf(int level) { return level >= 1 ? 1 : 2; }
public static boolean goToAllowed(int level) { return level == 0; }
public static double kShare(int level) { return level >= 2 ? 0.5 : 1.0; }
public static boolean virtualGuns(int level) { return level < 3; }
```

- A tick over 70% of the allowance sheds one level for the next tick.
- A skipped turn sheds one level for the rest of the round.
- Level 1: surf one wave. Level 2: also halve k. Level 3: also stop scoring virtual guns.

Decisions follow what happened, never the clock or the round number (DIAL-2).

---

## Learn the real allowance (TIME-3)

The adapter guesses 3 ms, the bench machine's constant. A rumble client may be quicker.

The first skipped turn proves the guess was too high. From then on the tick that caused it is the allowance.

```java
if (learnedAllowanceNanos <= 0 && lastUsedNanos > 0
        && (guessedAllowanceNanos < 0
            || lastUsedNanos >= MIN_LEARNED_SHARE * guessedAllowanceNanos)) {
    learnedAllowanceNanos = lastUsedNanos;
}
```

The floor (`MIN_LEARNED_SHARE`, 0.2) exists because the adapter times only its own call. A skip can follow an unrelated short tick, and that must not become a battle-long allowance.

---

## Last resort: duress (RES-9)

After three skipped turns in a round, the rest of the round runs in duress: no waves, no samples, no tree. Orbit the enemy, fire head-on at power 1 when the gun is cool, keep the radar on it.

```java
seed = 0x9E3779B97F4A7C15L ^ (round * 0xBF58476D1CE4E5B9L);
// ...
private int nextInt(int bound) {      // xorshift
    seed ^= seed << 13;
    seed ^= seed >>> 7;
    seed ^= seed << 17;
    return (int) Long.remainderUnsigned(seed, bound);
}
```

Reversals are pseudo-random but seeded from the round, so a replay is identical. A robot that cannot finish a tick dies when it stops moving, not when it aims badly.

---

## The session bench

Every bench so far forked a fresh JVM per battle against at most 60 opponents. A rumble client runs a session in one process against hundreds.

R7 added a session: 300 battles, one engine process, a control robot (sample.Tracker) beside Hadur.

| Battles | Skipped turns per battle |
|---|---|
| 1 to 25 | 10.0 |
| 101 to 125 | 39.5 |
| 276 to 300 | 89.6 |

The control stayed at 0.0 to 0.1. Survival, heap and live classes were flat.

Source: `docs/bench/r7-session.md`.

---

## Where the skips were

Skips were classified by position against each round's record: start, mid-round, decided.

| Battles | start | mid | decided |
|---|---|---|---|
| 1 to 25 | 0.0 | 4.1 | 5.9 |
| 276 to 300 | 0.0 | 3.9 | 85.8 |

Mid-round skips stayed flat. All the growth sat on the decided turn, one tick after a round ended: the profile save. Wiping the data directory before each battle held skips flat; a fresh JVM per battle with the directory kept grew the same way.

Source: `docs/skipped-turns-plan.md`, section 2.

---

## The cause

The round-end save walked the data directory.

- `bytesUsed` listed every file.
- Above 90% of the quota it read and decoded every profile of its own version to find seeds to drop.

That is work proportional to the number of opponents met, and a client that has met most of the field has hundreds. Prefilling 1,000 stats-only profiles cost 21 to 31 skipped turns per save; 2,000 cost 42 to 53.

Past about 700 profiles (275 bytes each, 200 KB quota) every save was skipped ("275 bytes do not fit"): nothing persisted at all (L-27).

---

## The fix: one listing, an index (R8)

MEM-11 to MEM-13: no save does work proportional to the files on disk.

```java
private Map<String, long[]> index() {
    if (index == null) adopt(list(dir));
    return index;
}
```

- The directory is listed once per battle, into an index of length and modification time.
- Writes and deletes keep the index current.
- Only files bigger than any stats-only profile are ever read to look for seeds (MEM-12).
- A save that still does not fit forgets the least recently written whole profiles (MEM-13).

Test: `listsOnce`. A file added behind the store's back is not seen, because nothing lists again.

---

## Did it work

R8 bench, 4 weak bots, 1 ms client constant, a directory of 698 stats-only profiles (docs/bench/r8-memory-scale.md):

| Jar | Skipped turns per battle |
|---|---|
| 3.3 | 188 to 224 |
| 3.4 | 10 to 26 |

3.4 did not slide live: its mean APS held 86.2 to 86.7 for four hours after an early dip (L-29).

The honest caveat: L-27 says the directory explains the lost memory and the skipped-turn growth, not necessarily the whole slide, and L-28 (three silent freezes) is still open.

---

## Recap

- Time the work at the edge; keep the core clock-free.
- Shed work in ordered levels, driven by events.
- Have a last-resort mode that is simple, bounded and deterministic.
- A cost that grows with data shows up only when the data grows. Bench for that.
- Never let a routine operation scale with everything you have ever stored.

Next: the note walks the classes, then the lab reproduces the slide in miniature.

